{"name":"Spectra","hex":"#375d4f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spectra","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of spectrum"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spectra"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spectrum","blocks":[{"text":"A spectrum is a set of related ideas, objects, or properties whose features overlap such that they blend to form a continuum. The word spectrum was first used scientifically in optics to describe the rainbow of colors in visible light after passing through a prism. In the optical spectrum, light wavelength is viewed as continuous, and spectral colors are seen to blend into one another smoothly when organized in order of their corresponding wavelengths. As scientific understanding of light advanced, the term came to apply to the entire electromagnetic spectrum, including radiation not visible to the human eye."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Rainbow_above_Kaviskis_Lake%2C_Lithuania.jpg/330px-Rainbow_above_Kaviskis_Lake%2C_Lithuania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectrum","wikidata":"Q654182"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Rainbow_above_Kaviskis_Lake%2C_Lithuania.jpg/500px-Rainbow_above_Kaviskis_Lake%2C_Lithuania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Rainbow_above_Kaviskis_Lake%2C_Lithuania.jpg/500px-Rainbow_above_Kaviskis_Lake%2C_Lithuania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The spectrum in a rainbow","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARainbow_above_Kaviskis_Lake%2C_Lithuania.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Political-spectrum-multiaxis.png/500px-Political-spectrum-multiaxis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conten